## Initial Q&A
### When was the most recent official population census conducted in Nigeria, and what urban population percentage did it record?
Answer: The most recent official population census in Nigeria was conducted in 2006, which recorded approximately 40% of the population as living in urban areas. The search result provided contains UN population estimates and ratios, not data from an official national census.

Source URL: https://www.macrotrends.net/countries/NGA/nigeria/urban-population

### What definition of "urban area" does the World Bank use when calculating urban population percentages for Nigeria?
Answer: The World Bank does not use a single, specific definition of "urban area" for Nigeria or other countries; instead, it relies on the definitions provided by national statistical offices. As stated in the search result, the urban population data is "calculated using World Bank population estimates and urban ratios from the United Nations World Urbanization Prospects," where those ratios are based on "urban areas as defined by national statistical offices." Therefore, for Nigeria, the definition used would be whatever criteria the Nigerian government applies in its national statistics.

Source URL: https://www.macrotrends.net/countries/NGA/nigeria/urban-population

### What definition of "urban area" does the United Nations use when calculating urban population percentages for Nigeria?
Answer: The United Nations uses the "Degree of Urbanization" framework, endorsed by the UN Statistical Commission in March 2020. For international comparisons, it defines "cities" as "a population of at least 50,000 inhabitants in contiguous dense grid cells (>1,500 inhabitants per km2)" and "towns and semi-dense areas" as "a population of at least 5,000 inhabitants in contiguous grid cells with a density of at least 300 inhabitants per km2." This method uses population size and density thresholds rather than national administrative criteria.

Source URL: https://blogs.worldbank.org/sustainablecities/how-do-we-define-cities-towns-and-rural-areas

### Is the 52% figure for Nigeria's urban population consistent with any recognized international demographic dataset or projection for the year 2020?
Answer: Yes, the 52% figure is consistent with recognized international data. Based on World Bank and United Nations estimates cited by Macrotrends, Nigeria's urban population in 2020 was 108,242,753. When combined with the implied total population from growth trends (around 206 million for 2020), this yields an urban share of approximately 52.5%, aligning closely with the 52% claim.

